PLACES=[
N('danville-state-hospital-pa','Danville State Hospital (State Hospital for the Insane at Danville)','Danville','pac','inst','standing',40.94854,-76.5957,'exact','view','drive','','1868–',
  "Pennsylvania's third public hospital for the mentally ill. The Assembly authorised it in 1868 for the northern part of the state, and it took its first patients in 1872 as the State Hospital for the Insane at Danville, on 250 acres bought partly with money raised by the townspeople. John McArthur Jr. drew the Kirkbride-plan main building, built of stone in 1868–1874 by C. S. Boaks. On the evening of March 5, 1881, a gas explosion started a fire that gutted the administration section, all the women's wards and part of the men's; John Sunderland led the rebuilding in brick, which the Society of Architectural Historians dates 1881–1883 and Wikipedia says took five years. The patient count peaked at 2,801 in November 1955. It is still a state psychiatric hospital.",
  "The Kirkbride building stands and is still used: a tall central administration block with arched entrances, a pediment and a square tower tapering to a spire, between four-storey cross blocks with pyramid-roofed towers, joined by three-storey wings, all laid out symmetrically so each ward looked out on the grounds. The architectural historians' entry notes warning signs and razor wire round the site. Interior not checked.",
  '','200 State Hospital Drive, about a mile southeast of Danville. An active state psychiatric hospital of the Department of Human Services: look from the public road only.',
  [W('Danville State Hospital'),[SAH+': Danville State Hospital','https://sah-archipedia.org/buildings/PA-02-MT14'],KB],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='John McArthur Jr.; John Sunderland',builder='C. S. Boaks',
  archw="SAH Archipedia: designed by John McArthur Jr. and built 1868–1874 with C. S. Boaks as builder; John Sunderland led the rebuilding after the 1881 fire"),
N('warren-state-hospital-pa','Warren State Hospital (State Hospital for the Insane at Warren)','North Warren','paw','inst','standing',41.8795,-79.14646,'site','view','drive','','1873–',
  "A Kirkbride-plan state hospital on the west bank of Conewango Creek, two miles northeast of Warren. The Society of Architectural Historians dates it 1873–1882, built under Thomas Kirkbride's direction with John Sunderland as contractor and supervising architect; Wikipedia names John McArthur Jr. as its designer and gives 1880 as the year it was established. The middle section with its twin towers went up in 1876–1880. Later came annexes by Green and Wicks (1898–1913), the Stone Building for acute cases by Eric Fisher Wood (1927–1930) and an X-shaped admissions building (1950–1952). The population peaked at 2,562 in 1947; Wikipedia says the hospital was still open in 2024.",
  "The main building is of golden sandstone (Olean conglomerate) quarried in two nearby valleys: a four-storey centre with twin towers and long rows of windows, between recessed fourteen-bay, three-storey wings. A chapel dedicated in 1973 has an altar cut from a five-ton sandstone block found on the grounds, and eighteen small brick staff houses of 1906 to the 1960s face State Street. The historians' entry says every building is used by state or county agencies or tenants. Interior not checked.",
  '','Main Drive off US 62 (North State Street), North Warren, Conewango Township. An active state hospital: look from the public road only.',
  [W('Warren State Hospital'),[SAH+': Warren State Hospital','https://sah-archipedia.org/buildings/PA-01-WA9'],KB],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='John McArthur Jr.; John Sunderland',
  archw="Wikipedia: designed by John McArthur Jr.; SAH Archipedia: John Sunderland was contractor and supervising architect"),
N('dixmont-state-hospital-pa','Dixmont State Hospital site (Reed Hall)','Kilbuck Township','paw','inst','gone',40.51583,-80.11222,'site','view','drive','','1859–2006',
  "Begun as the Department of the Insane of the Western Pennsylvania Hospital, which bought farmland on a hill above the Ohio River after Dorothea Dix argued against building in the city. The cornerstone of the Kirkbride building was laid on July 19, 1859, and it opened in 1862; the west wing was finished in 1868. The main building, Reed Hall, named for the first superintendent, Dr. Joseph A. Reed, had its outer wings swept forward to face the river view; Joseph W. Kerr was its architect, and it was put on the National Register in 1980. The hospital closed in July 1984, fires wrecked the empty buildings, and the state sold the 407 acres in 1999. Demolition for a shopping centre to be anchored by a Walmart began in 2005 and was finished in 2006; the digging then set off a landslide on September 19, 2006, that buried Route 65 and the railroad below for weeks.",
  "Reed Hall and the old buildings are gone; Wikipedia says the only building left is the Cammarata Building of 1971, a plain modern block. The state still owns the hospital cemetery up the hill, where about 1,300 graves are marked only by numbered stones, in rows that are slowly sliding. In 2013 the cut hillside was still being stabilised and the shopping centre was unlikely to be built.",
  '','Off Huntington Road, west of Emsworth, Kilbuck Township. Private land over an unstable slope, with nothing to visit: look from the road. The cemetery is state-owned; no source read gives rules for visiting it.',
  [W('Dixmont State Hospital'),['National Register record: Reed Hall','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/80003401'],
   ['TribLive (2013): Kilbuck landslide site still not stabilized','https://archive.triblive.com/local/pittsburgh-allegheny/kilbuck-landslide-site-still-not-stabilized-7-years-later'],
   ['Waymarking: Dixmont State Hospital Cemetery','https://waymarking.com/waymarks/WM1TP0_Dixmont_State_Hospital_Cemetery'],KB],
  brick='?',arch='Joseph W. Kerr',archw='The National Register record for Reed Hall names Kerr, Joseph W. as architect'),
N('kirkbrides-hospital-pa',"Kirkbride's Hospital (Institute of the Pennsylvania Hospital)",'Philadelphia','pae','inst','standing',39.96097,-75.21783,'exact','view','walk','','1856–1997',
  "Pennsylvania Hospital moved its insane patients out of its crowded wards at 8th and Spruce Streets to a 101-acre farm in West Philadelphia, where Isaac Holden's asylum opened in 1841. Its first superintendent, Thomas Story Kirkbride, worked out there the hospital plan that bears his name and was copied across the country. Overcrowding led to a second building for men along 49th Street, designed by Kirkbride's friend Samuel Sloan in a Neoclassical style: the cornerstone was laid on July 7, 1856, and it was finished in 1859. The 1841 building was pulled down in 1959. The Institute closed in 1997, when Pennsylvania Hospital sold the land; the 1859 building, a National Historic Landmark since 1965 (1966 by the Park Service's record), survives.",
  "The 1859 men's hospital stands, its domed centre still plain to see behind 20th-century additions (a one-storey auditorium and a three-storey wing in front). The Park Service says the original building remains much as built. It is now the Kirkbride Center, part of the Blackwell Human Services Campus. Interior not checked.",
  '','111 North 49th Street, at 49th and Haverford, Mill Creek, West Philadelphia. The Park Service lists it as not open to the public: look from the street.',
  [W('Institute of the Pennsylvania Hospital'),['National Park Service: Kirkbride\'s Hospital','https://www.nps.gov/places/kirkbrides-hospital.htm'],
   ['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/66000684'],KB],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='Samuel Sloan',archw="Wikipedia: the Department for Males building was designed by Samuel Sloan, Kirkbride's friend and collaborator"),
N('friends-hospital-pa','Friends Hospital (Frankford Asylum)','Philadelphia','pae','inst','standing',40.02636,-75.10217,'exact','view','drive','','1813–',
  "Founded by Quakers in 1813 as The Asylum for the Relief of Persons Deprived of the Use of Their Reason, later the Frankford Asylum for the Insane: Wikipedia calls it the first private mental hospital in the United States and the oldest with an unbroken history. Its main building, Scattergood Hall, copies the York Retreat that William Tuke designed in England in 1794, as the Quaker minister Thomas Scattergood proposed. Lodges for disturbed patients were added at the ends in 1827–1828, and the front has hardly changed since. The campus is a National Historic Landmark district (1999) and is still a psychiatric hospital.",
  "Scattergood Hall is three storeys over a raised basement of coursed granite, its walls stuccoed over stone; behind the front it has been added to again and again. Round it are the carriage house of 1885, Lawnside (the 1859 fieldstone superintendent's house by the old entrance), Greystone (1910–1911) and other old buildings, and about 100 acres of lawns, gardens and woods with more than 20,000 azaleas said to descend from one discarded plant; the Register form tells of public Garden Days held each spring since 1938. When the gardens now open was not checked.",
  '','4641 Roosevelt Boulevard, Northeast Philadelphia, beside Tacony Creek. An active private psychiatric hospital: look from the boulevard unless the hospital says the gardens are open.',
  [W('Friends Hospital'),['National Register record and form (1999)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/99000629']],
  brick='no'),
N('norristown-state-hospital-pa','Norristown State Hospital (State Lunatic Hospital at Norristown)','Norristown','pae','inst','standing',40.13365,-75.34867,'site','view','walk','','1878–',
  "Ground was broken on March 12, 1878, for a state hospital to relieve the insane wards of the Philadelphia Almshouse and the Harrisburg asylum; John Rice had the $600,000 contract for seven wards and the administration building, designed by the Wilson Brothers & Company in red brick High Victorian Gothic. It took patients from July 12, 1880, in echelon wards on both sides of the administration building, and until 1896 the women's side was run by a woman physician, Dr. Alice Bennett. It is still a state hospital, now mostly for forensic patients. In 2023 the state deeded about 70 acres of the grounds to the borough and county, and in 2025 a developer signed to build some 700 homes there.",
  "About thirty buildings survive on 225 acres, some leased to social-service agencies and some empty and decaying; Wikipedia singles out the derelict Female Admission Building at the front entrance, which the fire department has used for practice. Which of the 1878–1880 buildings still stand was not confirmed. The fields where patients once raised crops and livestock are now the neighbouring Norristown Farm Park, a public park.",
  '','Off Sterigere Street, on the Norristown and West Norriton line. An active state hospital with a forensic unit, and part of the grounds is a redevelopment site: look from the public streets only.',
  [W('Norristown State Hospital'),['WHYY (2019): redevelopment coming for large parcel','https://whyy.org/articles/redevelopment-coming-for-large-parcel-of-norristown-state-hospital-grounds'],
   ['Patch (2025): redevelopment signed','https://patch.com/pennsylvania/norristown/norristown-state-hospital-redevelopment-signed-700-new-homes-coming']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='Wilson Brothers & Company',builder='John Rice',
  archw='Wikipedia: designed 1878–1880 by the local firm of Wilson Brothers & Company; John Rice held the construction contract'),
N('pennhurst-state-school-pa','Pennhurst State School and Hospital','Spring City','pae','inst','standing',40.193717,-75.560162,'site','open','drive','','1903–1987',
  "The Eastern Pennsylvania State Institution for the Feeble-Minded and Epileptic, authorised in 1903 and built from 1903 to 1908 on Crab Hill at Spring City; the first resident came on November 23, 1908. Its cottage-plan buildings were joined by tunnels with walkways on top, and in 1930 a separate upper campus for women was finished. In a suit brought against it, a federal district court ruled for the first time that an institution must be closed on a constitutional right to community services, and it shut on December 9, 1987. The upper campus became the Southeastern Veterans' Center; the lower campus was sold to a developer, and since about 2010 the administration building has housed a seasonal haunted attraction, a use that Wikipedia calls controversial among neighbours and people once connected with Pennhurst.",
  "The older buildings, by Philip H. Johnson, are two-storey red brick with terra cotta and granite trim; the administration building has two side porte-cocheres, a front portico and a copper cupola. Daytime history tours walk past sixteen lower-campus buildings and into some cleaned-up rooms. Several buildings have come down: Hershey, Rockwell and the dietary building were demolished in 2020, and demolition on the upper campus began in 2016.",
  '','601 North Church Street, Spring City. Privately owned; the attraction sells tickets for its autumn haunted house and for daytime history and photography tours (about three hours) on its own site. The rest of the grounds are closed. The upper campus is the state veterans\' home.',
  [W('Pennhurst State School and Hospital'),['Pennhurst Asylum: daytime tours','https://pennhurstasylum.com/?p=1936']],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',bcol='red',hours='?',arch='Philip H. Johnson',
  archw='Wikipedia: the older buildings were designed by Phillip H. Johnson (so spelled there); George Lovatt designed several after 1937'),
N('byberry-state-hospital-pa','Philadelphia State Hospital at Byberry site','Philadelphia','pae','inst','gone',40.11771,-74.99238,'area','view','drive','','1906–2006',
  "A small work farm for the mentally ill in the Somerton section, on both sides of Roosevelt Boulevard at the Bucks County line, that grew into a cottage-plan city hospital: building began in 1906 and it opened in 1907. The main buildings went up between 1907 and the mid-1920s, and more between 1940 and 1953, over fifty in all, joined by tunnels. The population passed 7,000 in 1960, and photographs of its wards taken secretly by a conscientious objector, Charlie Lord, and printed in Life in May 1946 caused a national outcry; the state had taken it over in 1936. Repeated inspections condemned it, and it closed in June 1990. The empty buildings were wrecked and burned over the next fifteen years; Westrum Development bought the land, and a ceremony on June 14, 2006, marked the end of the demolition.",
  "Nothing of the hospital is left: The Arbours at Eagle Pointe, a community of some 330 homes, was built on the site. The pin is on a street in that development; the hospital's buildings stood on either side of the boulevard here.",
  '','Private homes off Roosevelt Boulevard (US 1), Somerton, Northeast Philadelphia. Nothing to visit: the streets are private residential ones.',
  [W('Philadelphia State Hospital at Byberry')],brick='?'),
N('allentown-state-hospital-pa','Allentown State Hospital site','Allentown','pane','inst','gone',40.61507,-75.42997,'site','view','drive','','1901–2010',
  "Planning began in 1901 and the hospital opened on October 3, 1912, at a cost of $1,931,270, serving Lehigh, Northampton, Carbon, Monroe and Pike counties. The population peaked at 2,012 in 1950. In November 1998 it became, says Wikipedia, the first psychiatric hospital in the country to do away with seclusion. It closed on December 17, 2010; the state sold it to be demolished, and the main building and nearly everything else came down from December 28, 2020, into 2021. The developer City Center plans a district called Northridge on the 195 acres: about 625 townhouses, offices, a small hospital, a park and a school, with at least 70 acres kept for recreation.",
  "Two of the hospital's buildings remain, used as the headquarters of Community Services for Children (Head Start). The rest is cleared ground waiting for building.",
  '','1600 Hanover Avenue, at Northridge Avenue, east Allentown. A private development site: look from the road.',
  [W('Allentown State Hospital'),['Lehigh Valley Public Media (2024): master plan approved','https://www.lehighvalleypublicmedia.org/news/k-12/officials-approve-master-plan-school-for-northridge-development-at-old-allentown-state-hospital/']],
  brick='?'),
N('mayview-state-hospital-pa','Mayview State Hospital site (Marshalsea)','South Fayette Township','paw','inst','gone',40.327,-80.1093,'area','view','drive','','1893–2016',
  "The City of Pittsburgh opened a poor farm and asylum here in 1893 and named it Marshalsea, after the London debtors' prison. Elise Mercur designed a children's hospital for it in 1897, and a fire on July 31, 1907, swept the laundry and annex. Renamed the Pittsburgh City Home and Hospital at Mayview in 1916, it had 4,200 patients by 1934, and the state took it over on June 1, 1941. Its forensic patients went to Torrance in November 2008 and it closed at the end of that year. The state sold the 335 acres in June 2010. Patch reported crews tearing the 39 buildings down in July 2012 for a business park; Wikipedia gives 2016 for the demolition and says the land is for housing.",
  "No hospital building is known to stand. Patch noted a large brick barn beside the Community and Recreation Center in Upper St. Clair as the last piece of the hospital's farm.",
  '','Mayview Road, near Bridgeville. Private land: nothing to visit.',
  [W('Mayview State Hospital'),['Patch (2012): crews demolish Mayview State Hospital','https://patch.com/pennsylvania/chartiersvalley/crews-demolish-mayview-state-hospital']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('embreeville-state-hospital-pa','Embreeville State Hospital site (Chester County Asylum)','West Bradford Township','pae','inst','gone',39.94650,-75.72707,'site','view','drive','','1898–2022',
  "Chester County's poorhouse and asylum for the insane, on a bend of Brandywine Creek. Asylum Projects says the county began building here in 1898 and took patients from Norristown by 1900; the state took the hospital over under the 1938 Full State Care Act and named it Embreeville State Hospital, and Wikipedia says it closed in 1980 (some buildings later held a youth detention centre). A developer bought the roughly 225 acres about 2013, and after a six-year fight West Bradford Township agreed in 2019 to pay $22.5 million on condition that every building be demolished and the ground cleaned of asbestos, mould and soil contamination. The township took the land in 2024. Asylum Projects gives 2022 for the demolition.",
  "The hospital buildings have been taken down, and the township is turning the 196 acres into a park: athletic fields, walking trails, pavilions and gardens, with a new road from Route 162. The county paupers' burial ground nearby, now in Cheslen Preserve, has rows of plain grey stone tablets.",
  '','1818 West Strasburg Road (Route 162). Owned by West Bradford Township. Its October 2025 update said the site was not yet open, with gated entrances on Strasburg Road, and should be open to the public by mid-2027.',
  [W('Embreeville, Pennsylvania'),['West Bradford Township: Embreeville revitalization','https://westbradford.org/334/Embreeville-Redevelopment'],
   ['Vista.Today (2019): settlement, hospital to be demolished','https://vista.today/2019/11/west-bradford-township-settles-with-developer-embreeville-hospital-to-finally-be-demolished/'],
   ['Asylum Projects: Embreeville State Hospital','https://asylumprojects.org/index.php/Embreeville_Complex']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('laurelton-state-village-pa','Laurelton State Village','Laurelton','pac','inst','standing',40.904816,-77.21428,'site','view','drive','','1913–1998',
  "Authorised by the legislature in 1913 as an institution for women, the Pennsylvania Village for Feeble-Minded Women took its first patient in January 1920 in western Union County; men were not admitted until 1969. It held more than 1,000 at its peak. The Public Works Administration added two cottages and administration, recreation and hospital buildings, finished in May 1939. The state closed it in 1998 and sold it in 2006; plans for a convention centre and dude ranch had come to nothing by 2011, and a new owner bought the 266 acres in 2021.",
  "Thirty-two buildings stand empty on overgrown grounds. The 1939 administration building has reinforced concrete walls faced with local stone, wood trim and a slate roof. In March 2024 the Daily Item reported thirteen people cited for trespassing here, and a two-alarm fire the July before that destroyed the empty Cottage No. 2.",
  '','Spruce and Hemlock Drives, off Route 235 west of Laurelton, Hartley Township. Private: a metal gate at the entrance and signs saying no admittance. Look from Route 235 only.',
  [W('Laurelton, Pennsylvania'),['Living New Deal: Laurelton Center','https://livingnewdeal.org/projects/old-laurelton-center-laurelton-pa/'],
   ['The Daily Item via Yahoo News (2024): 13 cited for trespassing','https://www.yahoo.com/news/13-people-cited-trespassing-former-171900209.html']],
  brick='?'),
N('polk-state-school-pa','Polk State School (Polk Center)','Polk','paw','inst','standing',41.37124,-79.93085,'site','view','drive','','1894–2023',
  "The state commissioned a home for the \"feeble minded\" in 1893 and built it on about 773 acres round the McKissick farm, six miles west of Franklin. Frederick J. Osterling of Pittsburgh designed it in 1894, and it was occupied in 1897; Murdoch Hall (1902–1903) and the school hall and auditorium (about 1915) followed. At its peak in 1953 it had 102 buildings and 3,490 residents in a place meant for 1,200. Renamed Polk Center in the 1970s, it closed as a state institution on May 1, 2023. In January 2025 it stood shut, costing the state $15.3 million a year to keep.",
  "A large campus of red brick buildings with fat round turrets and arched windows: the administration building mixes an Ionic pedimented porch with round corner towers under conical roofs and steep roofs; behind it are large residential halls and round one-storey meeting rooms with conical roofs. Interior not checked.",
  '','North of US 62 in Polk borough, Venango County. State-owned and closed: look from the road.',
  [[SAH+': Polk Center','https://sah-archipedia.org/buildings/PA-01-VE11'],W('Polk, Pennsylvania'),
   ['Explore Jefferson (2023): Polk Center closes','https://explorejeffersonpa.com/?p=340527'],
   ['Republican & Herald via Yahoo News (2025): vacant state properties','https://www.yahoo.com/news/maintaining-vacant-properties-costs-state-234600065.html']],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',arch='Frederick J. Osterling',
  archw='SAH Archipedia: designed by Frederick J. Osterling of Pittsburgh (1894), with Samuel D. Brady and Louis Stevens on the early 20th-century additions'),
N('retreat-state-hospital-pa','Retreat State Hospital (Luzerne County Almshouse, later SCI Retreat)','Newport Township','pane','inst','standing',41.18811,-76.07997,'site','view','drive','','1878–2021',
  "The Central Poor District of Luzerne County opened an almshouse beside the Susquehanna in 1878 and added a hospital for the insane in 1900; for thirty years it was the Retreat Hospital for the Insane and Almshouse. The county ran it until the state took it over in 1943. The hospital closed in 1981 and was made into a state prison, SCI Retreat (opened in January 1988 by the Times Leader; 1986 by Wikipedia), which closed in June 2020 by Asylum Projects or June 2021 by the Times Leader. In 2025 the state was preparing to sell the 264 acres.",
  "Twenty-three structures stand empty, among them cell blocks, a boiler plant and a chapel. Which date from the almshouse and hospital was not confirmed.",
  '','600 Retreat Road, about 12 miles south of Wilkes-Barre. State-owned and vacant: look from the road.',
  [W('State Correctional Institution – Retreat'),['Times Leader via Yahoo News (2025): county not buying Retreat','https://www.yahoo.com/news/manager-luzerne-county-not-buying-192200276.html'],
   ['Asylum Projects: Retreat State Hospital','https://asylumprojects.org/index.php/Retreat_State_Hospital']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('farview-state-hospital-pa','Farview State Hospital (SCI Waymart)','Waymart','pane','inst','standing',41.5744,-75.4296,'site','view','drive','','1908–',
  "Pennsylvania's hospital for the criminally insane from 1909 to 1995, on the high ground west of Waymart that had been the Delaware and Hudson's Farview picnic ground. Asylum Projects says the legislature set it up on May 11, 1905, that it was built in 1908–1913 to plans by J. C. M. Shirk of Philadelphia, as a cottage plan of large brick buildings in a Jacobean style joined round courtyards, and that patients worked a farm of up to 307 acres. The first patients came in 1912. It was known for poor conditions and scandals. A state prison opened in its spare buildings on October 26, 1989, after the Camp Hill riots, and in October 1995 the hospital passed to the Department of Corrections as its forensic treatment centre.",
  "Now SCI Waymart, an active medium-security prison: 33 buildings inside the fence joined by corridors, with a garage, power plant, warehouse, staff houses and offices outside it. Which buildings are the hospital's was not confirmed.",
  '','11 Farview Drive, Canaan Township, outside Waymart. An active state prison: view from the public road only.',
  [W('State Correctional Institution – Waymart'),['Pennsylvania Department of Corrections: SCI Waymart','https://www.pa.gov/agencies/cor/state-prisons/sci-waymart'],
   ['Asylum Projects: Farview State Hospital','https://asylumprojects.org/index.php/SCI_Waymart']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='J. C. M. Shirk; Charles L. Hillman',
  archw="Asylum Projects: the original buildings were designed by J.C.M. Shirk of Philadelphia, and after Shirk's death in 1918 his partner Charles L. Hillman designed the later ones"),
]
